  link { url = "/docs/records-enums", "Model data with records and enums" },
  link { url = "/docs/strings", "Work with Unicode text" },
  link { url = "/docs/gpu", "Run your first GPU program" },
  link { url = "/docs/language-reference", "Language reference" },
  link { url = "/docs/library", "Alphabetical function index" }
}


h2 { "Reuse an algorithm across types" }

A generic function declares type parameters in square brackets: fn identity[T](value: T) -> T returns its argument with return value. identity(42) works with i64 and identity("hello") works with String; the compiler specializes the same declaration for each type. For operations that require a named capability, declare a trait and constrain the type parameter, such as T: Add. Generic records and enums use the same square-bracket syntax.

list {
  link { url = "/docs/generics-traits", "Write generic functions, data types and explicit trait implementations" }
}
